# A Theoretical Framework for Deep Anomaly Detection in SETI: A Mathematical Approach

**Author:** Ouadi Maakoul  
**Proposed Degree:** Doctor of Philosophy in Applied Mathematics / Signal Processing  
**Date of Submission:** May 24, 2026  

---

## Abstract

The Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence (SETI) poses a unique anomaly detection problem: the target signal is unknown a priori, buried under non‑stationary interference, and must be extracted from exascale data streams. Existing algorithmic approaches lack formal guarantees on detection sensitivity, false positive control, and computational scalability. This dissertation develops a rigorous mathematical framework for technosignature detection, grounded in information theory, computational complexity theory, Bayesian decision theory, and statistical learning theory. Principal contributions include: (i) lower bounds on the time complexity of real‑time RFI mitigation as a function of data rate, bandwidth, and false positive constraints; (ii) generalization bounds for variational autoencoders in out‑of‑distribution signal detection; (iii) an optimal drift‑rate search strategy derived from calculus of variations; (iv) a Bayesian posterior probability for extraterrestrial origin with calibrated priors and decision‑theoretic optimal thresholds; and (v) a simulation‑to‑reality gap certificate via domain adaptation bounds. The framework is shown to be provably optimal within the specified computational and statistical constraints.

