## 2. Central Research Question and Mapping to Workstreams

**Central Research Question (CRQ).**  
Does there exist a computationally tractable, statistically consistent, and formally verifiable framework for detecting signals of unknown origin in exascale radio telescope data, with controlled false positive rate and guaranteed sensitivity to anomalies outside the training distribution?

The CRQ decomposes into four sub‑requirements, each addressed by a dedicated workstream:

| Requirement | Formal meaning | Workstream |
|-------------|----------------|-------------|
| (i) Computationally tractable | Time complexity \( O(R \cdot \text{poly}(\log(1/\alpha), 1/\tau^2)) \) | A – Complexity bounds |
| (ii) Statistically consistent | Detection power → 1 as observation time → ∞, for any \( Q \) with sufficiently high SNR | B – Generalization bounds |
| (iii) Formally verifiable | Explicit thresholds derived from Bayesian decision theory with calibrated priors | C + D – Drift sampling + Bayesian inference |
| (iv) Controlled false positive + guaranteed sensitivity | Bounds on \( \text{FPR} \) and \( \text{FNR} \) that hold even under distribution shift (simulation → reality) | E – Domain adaptation |

