## 4. Core Hypothesis

**Core Hypothesis.**  
A hierarchical Bayesian framework that integrates (i) computational complexity lower bounds (Theorem 1), (ii) information‑theoretic generalization bounds (Theorem 2), (iii) an optimal drift‑rate sampling strategy (Theorem 3), (iv) a Bayes decision rule with calibrated priors (Theorem 4), and (v) domain adaptation bounds (Theorem 6) yields a detector that is provably optimal within the constraints of finite computation and finite training data.

Specifically, for any \( \epsilon > 0 \), there exists an algorithm with:
- Time complexity \( O(R \cdot \text{poly}(\log(1/\alpha), 1/\tau^2)) \),
- Sample complexity \( n = O\left( \frac{d \cdot \log(1/\delta)}{\epsilon^2} \right) \) for the VAE training,
- Simulation fidelity requirement \( \delta = O(\epsilon) \),
such that the detection power is within \( \epsilon \) of the information‑theoretic optimum, and the false positive rate is bounded by \( \alpha + \epsilon \).

