## 7. Finite‑Sample Complexity and Feasibility

The core hypothesis includes explicit sample complexity bounds. For the VAE training (Workstream B), we require:

\[
n = O\left( \frac{d \cdot \log(1/\zeta)}{\epsilon^2} \right)
\]

samples from \( P_{\text{RFI}} \) to achieve an OOD generalization gap of at most \( \epsilon \) with probability \( 1-\zeta \). With \( d \approx 50 \) (typical latent dimension for spectrograms), \( \epsilon = 0.1 \), \( \zeta = 0.05 \), we obtain \( n \approx 50 \cdot \log(20) / 0.01 \approx 50 \cdot 3 / 0.01 = 15,000 \) samples. This is realistic given the petabytes of archival SETI data.

For the domain adaptation bound (Workstream E), the required size of the unlabeled real sample is:

\[
m = O\left( \frac{\text{VC}(\mathcal{H}) \cdot \log(1/\zeta)}{\epsilon^2} \right).
\]

For a convolutional neural network with \( \text{VC} \approx 10^5 \), \( \epsilon = 0.05 \), we get \( m \approx 10^5 \cdot \log(20) / 0.0025 \approx 10^5 \cdot 3 / 0.0025 = 1.2 \times 10^8 \) samples – large but still feasible with modern GPU clusters and the SKA’s high data rates.

