## 10. Significance and Impact

This dissertation provides the first mathematically rigorous treatment of the SETI anomaly detection problem. By grounding the framework in fundamental limits—computational, statistical, and information‑theoretic—it transforms SETI from a heuristic search into a well‑posed inference problem with provable guarantees. The results will have immediate applicability to the Square Kilometre Array (SKA) and other next‑generation telescopes, and will generalize to any anomaly detection task in high‑volume, non‑stationary signal environments (e.g., gravitational wave burst detection, neutrino telescope triggers, network intrusion detection).

---

## 11. Conclusion

We have proposed a comprehensive theoretical framework that addresses the six fundamental gaps in current SETI signal processing. The framework is built on a hierarchy of proven theorems, each providing a formal guarantee: computational tractability (Theorem 1), statistical consistency (Theorem 2), optimal resource allocation (Theorem 3), Bayesian decision‑theoretic thresholds (Theorem 4), scale invariance (Theorem 5), and robustness to distribution shift (Theorem 6). The unified framework is both theoretically sound and practically feasible, as demonstrated by the finite‑sample complexity calculations. This PhD will deliver the mathematical foundations that future technosignature detection pipelines will rely upon.

